// `python.lang.best-practice.open-never-closed.open-never-closed`),
// pinned at semgrep/semgrep-rules@9d73d08e70fee9fc1fd940d1378ca6c601312883
// python/lang/best-practice/open-never-closed.yaml. The redistributed
// target file is from a different upstream — TuGraph-family/tugraph-db
// at master commit 672e4b1998b78e5dbd45ae44950e86b48c841437,
// release/det_ver.py — which avoids the semgrep-rules license
// carve-out entirely (semgrep-rules ships under Semgrep Rules License
// v1.0; the rule's stable identity is the only thing we cite, and the
// labelled code is Apache-2.0 tugraph-db, not semgrep-rules itself).
// Mapping against cntrdct's pr-miner detector:
// - upstream line 5 (corpus line 9) defines `def get_ver():` at top
//   level, so spec F2's `function_definition` extractor reaches it
//   and produces the item set {open, readlines, find, split} (plus
//   duplicates collapsed). Spec F4's violation predicate requires the
//   mined rule {open} -> {close} to first emerge from spec F3's
//   Apriori pass at MIN_SUPPORT = 0.05 and MIN_CONFIDENCE = 0.85.
//   The audit corpus has only one Python function that contains both
//   `open` and `close` — `replace_ver` in this same file at upstream
//   line 17 (corpus 21) — so the mined-rule denominator is 1 of N
//   transactions, well below the confidence threshold. v0 therefore
//   never synthesises the rule, never runs F4 against `get_ver`, and
//   the bug stays unflagged: FN by mining sparsity, NOT by extractor
//   scope. Closing the 1.00 gap on pr-miner is a corpus-density
//   problem, not an extractor-widening problem — every additional
//   Python file in the audit corpus that uses paired open/close
//   pulls the mined-rule confidence above 0.85, after which v0
//   begins to flag `get_ver`. Separate engineering with its own
//   preregistration; the audit-harness contribution is documenting
//   the gap.
// Net effect on the audit: `pr-miner` enters the denominator at
// 0/1/0.00; the other five detectors are unchanged. Overall
// recall_upper_bound moves from 0.28 (8/29) to 0.27 (8/30) — a
// shallow drop because pr-miner adds only one expected entry, not
// because the existing detectors regressed. The drop is downward
// for the right reason: a new detector entered the corpus with a
// 0-TP single-source bound by detector design (mining sparsity at
// the v0 corpus density), exactly the kind of signal Heckman &
// Williams IST 2011 motivates for the audit harness.
